Credential Access – Logon Case Investigation
1. Logon Activity

The investigation was started by searching for the keyword logon in:

Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational

The identified logon activity was legitimate. During the investigation, PowerShell execution was also observed. The event was associated with Task ID 106.

2. PowerShell Execution

A search for Task ID 106 showed PowerShell executing an encoded command.

The encoded command was decoded using CyberChef for further investigation.

The decoded PowerShell script was found to:

Modify PowerShell Script Block Logging settings.
Modify AMSI-related functionality.
Create a System.Net.WebClient object.
Set a User-Agent value.
Connect to an internal IP address.
Request /admin/get.php.
Process downloaded data using a custom RC4-like routine.
Pass the processed data to IEX for execution.
3. IP Address and URL Analysis

The following Base64 value was identified:

aAB0AHQAcAA6AC8ALwAxADAALgAxADAALgAxADAALgA1AA==

After decoding:

http://10.10.10.5

The script also contains:

$t='/admin/get.php'

The complete requested resource is:

http://10.10.10.5/admin/get.php

4. get.php Investigation

A search was performed for get.php to determine whether the requested resource was accessed and whether any activity occurred afterward.

Based on the provided logs, no further activity was observed after the access attempt.

5. Overall Activity

The logon activity was followed by PowerShell execution containing an encoded command. After decoding the command, it was observed that PowerShell attempted to access:

http://10.10.10.5/admin/get.php

The script also contained PowerShell logging and AMSI-related modifications and functionality to process downloaded data.

No post-access activity was observed in the provided evidence.
